{:edb/db-before-t 854 :edb/db-after-t 855 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534167 50 #inst "2026-10-11T17:25:27.608Z" 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186151846 1071 "Which of the following integers belong to the residue class $[5]_{8}$?\n\n1. $-3$\n2. $13$\n3. $3$" 13194139534167 17592186045417 false] [17592186151846 1071 "Which of the following integers belong to the residue class $[5]_{8}?$\n\n- $-3$\n\n- $13$\n\n- $3$" 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186151846 1074 "The residue class $[5]_{8}$ consists of the elements that are congruent to $5$ modulo $8$. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[5]_{8} &= {x ∈ Z\\begin{vmatrix}x≡5(mod8)} \\\\ = {x ∈ Z \\mid x = 5 + 8k, k ∈ Z} \\\\ = {5 + 8k\\end{vmatrix}k ∈ Z} \\\\\n&= {…, 5 - 2 \\cdot 8, 5 - 8, 5, 5 + 8, 5 + 2 \\cdot 8, …} \\\\\n&= {…,-11,-3, 5, 13, 21, …}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $-3$ and $13$ belong to $[5]_{8}$, while $3$ does not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534167 17592186045417 false] [17592186151846 1074 "The residue class $[5]_{8}$ consists of the elements that are congruent to $5$ modulo $8.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[5]_{8} & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x=5+8k,\\,k\\in Z} \\\\  & ={5+8k\\,|\\,k\\in Z} \\\\  & ={…,\\,5-2\\cdot 8,\\,5-8,\\,5,\\,5+8,\\,5+2\\cdot 8,\\,…} \\\\  & ={…,-11,-3,5,13,21,…}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $-3$ and $13$ belong to $[5]_{8},$ while $3$ does not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186151847 1071 "Which of the following residue classes modulo $3$ contain the integer $7$?\n\n1. $[4]_3$\n2. $[-1]_3$\n3. $[1]_3$" 13194139534167 17592186045417 false] [17592186151847 1071 "Which of the following residue classes modulo $3$ contain the integer $7?$\n\n- $[4]_3$\n\n- $[-1]_3$\n\n- $[1]_3$" 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186151847 1074 "We have $7 \\in [a]_{3}$ if\n$7 \\equiv a \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:3)$.\n\nLet's now consider each of the given equivalence classes.\n\n- $7 \\in [4]_3$ because $7 \\equiv 4 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:3)$ $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n- $7 \\not\\in [-1]_3$ because $7 \\not\\equiv -1 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:3)$ $\\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n- $7 \\in [1]_3$ because $7 \\equiv 1 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:3)$ $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534167 17592186045417 false] [17592186151847 1074 "We have $7 \\in [a]_{3}$ if \n\n$$\n7 \\equiv a \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:3).\n$$\n\nLet's now consider each of the given equivalence classes.\n\n- $7 \\in [4]_3$ because $7 \\equiv 4 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:3)$ $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\n- $7 \\not\\in [-1]_3$ because $7 \\not\\equiv -1 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:3)$ $\\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\n- $7 \\in [1]_3$ because $7 \\equiv 1 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:3)$ $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186151848 1071 "If $[a]_n$ denotes the residue class containing all integers congruent to $a$ modulo $n$, then which of the following residue classes is equal to $[13]_3$?\n\n1. $[0]_3$\n2. $[1]_3$\n3. $[2]_3$" 13194139534167 17592186045417 false] [17592186151848 1071 "If $[a]_n$ denotes the residue class containing all integers congruent to $a$ modulo $n,$ then which of the following residue classes is equal to $[13]_3?$\n\n- $[0]_3$\n\n- $[1]_3$\n\n- $[2]_3$" 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186151848 1074 "We have\n\n$$\n[a]_{3} = [b]_{3}\n$$\n\nif\n$a \\equiv b \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:3)$.\n\nThe residue class $[13]_{3}$ consists of the elements that are congruent to $13$ modulo $3$.\n\nWe can find an alternative representation for this residue class as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[13]_{3} &= {x ∈ Z\\begin{vmatrix}x≡13(mod3)} \\\\ = {x ∈ Z \\mid x≡4 \\cdot 3 + 1(mod3)} \\\\ = {x ∈ Z \\mid x≡4 \\cdot 0 + 1(mod3)} \\\\ = {x ∈ Z\\end{vmatrix}x≡1(mod3)} \\\\\n&= [1]_{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139534167 17592186045417 false] [17592186151848 1074 "We have $[a]_{3} = [b]_{3}$ if \n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:3).\n$$\n\nThe residue class $[13]_{3}$ consists of the elements that are congruent to $13$ modulo $3.$ \n\nWe can find an alternative representation for this residue class as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[13]_{3} & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡13\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡4\\cdot 3+1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡4\\cdot 0+1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)} \\\\  & =[1]_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186151850 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- There are $4$ distinct residue classes modulo $8$\n\n- $[5]_{3} \\cap [9]_{3} = \\emptyset$\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z} = [4]_{3} \\cup [5]_{3} \\cup [9]_{3}$" 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186151850 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. There are $4$ distinct residue classes modulo $8$\n2. $[5]_{3} \\cap [9]_{3} = \\emptyset$\n3. $\\mathbb{Z} = [4]_{3} \\cup [5]_{3} \\cup [9]_{3}$" 13194139534167 17592186045417 false] [17592186151850 1074 "Recall that the residue classes modulo $n$ have the following properties:\n\n- There are exactly $n$ distinct residue classes modulo $n$\n\n- Two distinct residue classes have an empty intersection: $[a]_n \\cap [b]_n = \\emptyset$ if $a \\not\\equiv b \\: (\\text{mod}\\:n)$\n\n- Residue classes modulo $n$ form a partition of the set of all integers: \n\n$$\n\\mathbb{Z} = [0]_n \\cup [1]_n \\cup \\cdots \\cup [n-1]_n\n$$\n\n- The integers modulo $n$ is defined as the set containing all distinct residue classes modulo $n$: \n\n$$\n\\mathbb{Z}_n = \\big\\{[0]_n,\\, [1]_n,\\, \\ldots,\\,  [n-1]_n\\big\\}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. There are exactly $8$ distinct residue classes modulo $8.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since $5 \\not\\equiv 9 \\: (\\text{mod }3),$ we have that $[5]_3\\cap [9]_3= \\emptyset.$\n\n- Statement III is true. First, notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[4]_{3} & =[1+3\\cdot 1]_{3}=[1]_{3} \\\\ [5]_{3} & =[2+3\\cdot 1]_{3}=[2]_{3} \\\\ [9]_{3} & =[0+3\\cdot 3]_{3}=[0]_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[4]_{3}\\cup [5]_{3}\\cup [9]_{3} & =[1]_{3}\\cup [2]_{3}\\cup [0]_{3} \\\\  & =[0]_{3}\\cup [1]_{3}\\cup [2]_{3} \\\\  & =Z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186151850 1074 "Recall that the residue classes modulo $n$ have the following properties:\n\n- There are exactly $n$ distinct residue classes modulo $n$\n- Two distinct residue classes have an empty intersection: $[a]_n \\cap [b]_n = \\emptyset$ if $a \\not\\equiv b \\: (\\text{mod}\\:n)$\n- Residue classes modulo $n$ form a partition of the set of all integers: $\\mathbb{Z} = [0]_n \\cup [1]_n \\cup \\cdots \\cup [n-1]_n$\n- The integers modulo $n$ is defined as the set containing all distinct residue classes modulo $n$: $\\mathbb{Z}_n = \\big\\{[0]_n,\\, [1]_n,\\, \\ldots,\\, [n-1]_n\\big\\}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. There are exactly $8$ distinct residue classes modulo $8$.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since $5 \\not\\equiv 9 \\: (\\text{mod}3)$, we have that $[5]_3\\cap [9]_3= \\emptyset$.\n- Statement III is true. First, notice the following:\n$[4]_{3}|= [1 + 3 \\cdot 1]_{3} = [1]_{3}; [5]_{3}|= [2 + 3 \\cdot 1]_{3} = [2]_{3}; [9]_{3}|= [0 + 3 \\cdot 3]_{3} = [0]_{3}$\nAs a result, we get\n$[4]_{3}∪[5]_{3}∪[9]_{3}|= [1]_{3}∪[2]_{3}∪[0]_{3}; = [0]_{3}∪[1]_{3}∪[2]_{3}; = Z$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." 13194139534167 17592186045417 false] [17592186152097 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412964 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186152097 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412971 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186152097 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412972 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186152097 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412976 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186152098 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412965 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186152098 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412966 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186152098 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412969 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186152098 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412970 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186152098 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412975 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186152099 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412959 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186152099 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412960 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186152099 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412962 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186152099 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412967 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186152099 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412974 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186152100 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412961 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186152100 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412963 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186152100 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412968 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186152100 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412973 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186152100 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412977 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186152339 1050 540.0 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186152792 1068 #edb/ref 17592186151259 13194139534167 17592186045417 false] [17592186152792 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412948 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186152792 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186152792 1071 "Which of the following integers belong to the residue class $[6]_{9}$?\n\n1. $-12$\n2. $3$\n3. $24$" 13194139534167 17592186045417 false] [17592186152792 1071 "Which of the following integers belong to the residue class $[6]_{9}?$\n\n- $-12$\n- $3$\n- $24$" 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186152792 1074 "The residue class $[6]_{9}$ consists of the elements that are congruent to $6$ modulo $9.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[6]_{9} & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9)} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x=6+9k,\\,k\\in Z} \\\\  & ={6+9k\\,|\\,k\\in Z} \\\\  & ={…,\\,6-2\\cdot 9,\\,6-9,\\,6,\\,6+9,\\,6+2\\cdot 9,\\,…} \\\\  & ={…,-12,-3,6,15,24,…}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $-12$ and $24$ belong to $[6]_{9},$ while $3$ does not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186153456 1064 "Given an integer $a$, the **residue class** (or **congruence class**) of $a$ modulo $n$ is the set containing all integers congruent to $a$ (modulo $n$).\n\nThe residue class of $a$ modulo $n$, denoted $[a]_n$, can be expressed using a conditional definition as follows:\n\n$$\n[a]_n = \\big\\{x \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\: \\mid \\: x \\equiv a \\, \\: (\\text{mod}\\:n) \\big\\}\n$$\n\nPut another way, the residue class $[a]_n$ contains all integers that have the same remainder as $a$ when divided by $n$.\n\nFor example, the residue class of $0$ modulo $2$ contains all the even integers because they all have a remainder of zero when divided by $2$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[0]_{2} &= {x ∈ Z\\begin{vmatrix}x≡0(mod2)} \\\\ = {2k\\end{vmatrix}k ∈ Z} \\\\\n&= {…,-4,-2, 0, 2, 4…}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe residue class of $1$ modulo $2$ contains all the odd integers because they all have a remainder of one when divided by $2$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[1]_{2} &= {x ∈ Z\\begin{vmatrix}x≡1(mod2)} \\\\ = {2k + 1\\end{vmatrix}k ∈ Z} \\\\\n&= {…,-3,-1, 1, 3, 5…}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that each element of $\\mathbb Z$ lies either in $[0]_2$ or in $[1]_2$ (but never both). Thus, these residue classes form a *partition* of the integers. We'll call this partition $\\mathbb Z_2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbb Z_2 = \\big\\{[0]_2, [1]_2\\big\\}\n$$\n\nWe can visualize this partition using the diagram shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f84b296319e078df1827fe29dbd15fb61ce0d10cac69660f11d6a96d5c44478a.png)\n\nSimilarly, the integers can also be partitioned into five residue classes modulo $5$.\n\n$$\n\\mathbb Z_5 = \\big\\{[0]_5, [1]_5, [2]_5,[3]_5, [4]_5 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, each residue class contains all numbers that are congruent modulo $5$. We can visualize this partition of $\\mathbb Z$ using the diagram below.\n\n![](images/27/270f63afd4a0022e9f31a48e5ea5f4e799822e8c4eb5813e6c5e616e316c02e1.png)\n\nA residue class is a specific type of a more general object called an **equivalence class.** We'll learn more about different types of equivalence classes soon." 13194139534167 17592186045417 false] [17592186153456 1064 "Given an integer $a,$ the **residue class** (or **congruence class**) of $a$ modulo $n$ is the set containing all integers congruent to $a$ (modulo $n$). \n\nThe residue class of $a$ modulo $n,$ denoted $[a]_n,$ can be expressed using a conditional definition as follows:\n\n$$\n[a]_n = \\big\\{ x \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\: | \\: x \\equiv a \\,  \\: (\\text{mod}\\:n) \\big\\}\n$$\n\nPut another way, the residue class $[a]_n$ contains all integers that have the same remainder as $a$ when divided by $n.$\n\nFor example, the residue class of $0$ modulo $2$ contains all the even integers because they all have a remainder of zero when divided by $2.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0]_{2} & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)} \\\\  & ={2k\\,|\\,k\\in Z} \\\\  & ={…,-4,-2,0,2,4…}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe residue class of $1$ modulo $2$ contains all the odd integers because they all have a remainder of one when divided by $2.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[1]_{2} & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)} \\\\  & ={2k+1\\,|\\,k\\in Z} \\\\  & ={…,-3,-1,1,3,5…}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that each element of $\\mathbb Z$ lies either in $[0]_2$ or in $[1]_2$ (but never both). Thus, these residue classes form a *partition* of the integers. We'll call this partition $\\mathbb Z_2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbb Z_2 = \\big\\{[0]_2, [1]_2\\big\\}\n$$\n\nWe can visualize this partition using the diagram shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f84b296319e078df1827fe29dbd15fb61ce0d10cac69660f11d6a96d5c44478a.png)\n\nSimilarly, the integers can also be partitioned into five residue classes modulo $5.$ \n\n$$\n\\mathbb Z_5 = \\big\\{[0]_5, [1]_5, [2]_5,[3]_5, [4]_5 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, each residue class contains all numbers that are congruent modulo $5.$ We can visualize this partition of $\\mathbb Z$ using the diagram below.\n\n![](images/27/270f63afd4a0022e9f31a48e5ea5f4e799822e8c4eb5813e6c5e616e316c02e1.png)\n\nA residue class is a specific type of a more general object called an **equivalence class.** We'll learn more about different types of equivalence classes soon." 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186153459 1064 "Let's list some important properties of residue classes:\n\n- There are exactly $n$ *distinct* residue classes modulo $n$.\n- If $a \\equiv b \\: (\\text{mod}\\:n)$ then $[a]_n=[b]_n$. In particular, this allows us to pick any element of a class as its representative.\n- On the other hand, two distinct residue classes have an empty intersection:\n$a \\not\\equiv b \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:n) \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad [a]_n \\cap [b]_n = \\emptyset$\n- The residue classes modulo $n$ form a partition of the set of all integers. This means the integers can be constructed from the (disjoint) union of all distinct residue classes.\n$\\mathbb{Z} = [0]_n \\cup [1]_n \\cup \\cdots \\cup [n-1]_n$\n\nThe **integers modulo** $\\boldsymbol n$ is defined as the set containing all distinct residue classes modulo $n$:\n\n$$\n\\mathbb{Z}_n = \\big\\{[0]_n,\\, [1]_n,\\, \\ldots,\\, [n-1]_n\\big\\}\n$$\n\nThe set $\\mathbb Z_n$ has some interesting properties. We'll learn more about those in future lessons." 13194139534167 17592186045417 false] [17592186153459 1064 "Let's list some important properties of residue classes:\n\n- There are exactly $n$ *distinct* residue classes modulo $n.$\n\n- If $a \\equiv b \\: (\\text{mod}\\:n)$ then $[a]_n=[b]_n.$ In particular, this allows us to pick any element of a class as its representative.\n\n- On the other hand, two distinct residue classes have an empty intersection: \n\n$$\na \\not\\equiv b \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:n)\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n[a]_n \\cap [b]_n = \\emptyset\n$$\n\n- The residue classes modulo $n$ form a partition of the set of all integers. This means the integers can be constructed from the (disjoint) union of all distinct residue classes. \n\n$$\n\\mathbb{Z} = [0]_n \\cup [1]_n \\cup \\cdots \\cup [n-1]_n\n$$\n\nThe **integers modulo** $\\boldsymbol n$ is defined as the set containing all distinct residue classes modulo $n$: \n\n$$\n\\mathbb{Z}_n = \\big\\{[0]_n,\\, [1]_n,\\, \\ldots,\\,  [n-1]_n\\big\\}\n$$\n\nThe set $\\mathbb Z_n$ has some interesting properties. We'll learn more about those in future lessons." 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186153479 1064 "Let's go back to our diagram for visualizing the residue classes modulo ![](images/27/270d33581e0657a10d567c6cfaa529521b34e20a39c9a29550698cbf252fabfa.svg)\n\n![](images/f8/f84b296319e078df1827fe29dbd15fb61ce0d10cac69660f11d6a96d5c44478a.png)\n\nAny given representation of a residue class is not unique.\n\nFor example, the integers congruent to zero modulo ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) are usually denoted ![](images/7e/7e03abb8339cac8bc90200b99c4de9c414e179036e7b7defca80f7388c9fdb7c.svg) In this case, ![](images/15/156b31ede73de5301befe67f02745a0202f1806a62f9fdecc6fc9ee3f21117ba.svg) is the **representative** of the class:\n![](images/5f/5f298c564c0538edb6fc85abb585f5fef0b0bcff7fa704860794bb5681fb036d.svg)\n\nHowever, we can use *any* element of the class to represent the class. Here are some examples:\n\n![](images/38/383a513e20a70007e3d102dd4c8e9898550ac30514a3352842e1b9be8ad7601e.svg)\n\nWe can also use negative numbers as representatives of the class:\n\n![](images/01/014ec5b5f21d3b216343811bddb6d84c3a66c7b29baa09504d67cab71b83aea6.svg)\n\nWhat's important to understand is that *all of these sets are equal*:\n\n![](images/31/3155c29054f13a2b96de8be909ebd1ac6c98d50f64ea5d7a0acef3f1dcf48ac1.svg)\n\nThe same idea is true for ![](images/7a/7ac9fdeb690307bfa97c781ae9ace4d91bbf6f3b15cfe6dd7139225bdca44fc5.svg)\n\n![](images/cd/cd1101d39e228d23c551fcaa8ba377cd819661aaec6121706eab184658d2a854.svg)\n\nThe same idea applies to any residue class modulo ![](images/c2/c2475d72e6ec36042292de6c0160930174172b2386148d823a70e59fddfb93c2.svg) For example:\n\n![](images/0c/0c4d211a3b3f6238f9e647585d62904af4fd263fb79eb94e45e9b51d8b62abde.svg)\n\nDespite this non-uniqueness, we usually choose the smallest nonnegative element of a residue class to represent the class." 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186153479 1064 "Let's go back to our diagram for visualizing the residue classes modulo $2$.\n\n![](images/f8/f84b296319e078df1827fe29dbd15fb61ce0d10cac69660f11d6a96d5c44478a.png)\n\nAny given representation of a residue class is not unique.\n\nFor example, the integers congruent to zero modulo $2$ are usually denoted $[0]_2$. In this case, $0$ is the **representative** of the class:\n\n$$\n[0]_{2} = {x \\mid x≡0(mod2)}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can use *any* element of the class to represent the class. Here are some examples:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[2]_{2} &= {x\\begin{vmatrix}x≡2(mod2)} \\\\ [4]_{2}|= {x \\mid x≡4(mod2)} \\\\ [6]_{2}|= {x\\end{vmatrix}x≡6(mod2)} \\\\\n&= ⋮\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can also use negative numbers as representatives of the class:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[- 2]_{2} &= {x\\begin{vmatrix}x≡ - 2(mod2)} \\\\ [- 4]_{2}|= {x \\mid x≡ - 4(mod2)} \\\\ [- 6]_{2}|= {x\\end{vmatrix}x≡ - 6(mod2)} \\\\\n&= ⋮\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat's important to understand is that *all of these sets are equal*:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[0]_{2} &= [2]_{2} = [4]_{2} = [6]_{2} = ⋯ \\\\\n&= [- 2]_{2} = [- 4]_{2} = [- 6]_{2} = ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe same idea is true for $[1]_2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[1]_{2} &= [3]_{2} = [5]_{2} = [7]_{2} = ⋯ \\\\\n&= [- 1]_{2} = [- 3]_{2} = [- 5]_{2} = ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe same idea applies to any residue class modulo $n$. For example:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[0]_{5} &= [5]_{5} = [10]_{5} = ⋯ \\\\\n[1]_{5} &= [6]_{5} = [11]_{5} = ⋯ \\\\\n[2]_{5} &= [7]_{5} = [12]_{5} = ⋯ \\\\\n[3]_{5} &= [8]_{5} = [13]_{5} = ⋯ \\\\\n[4]_{5} &= [9]_{5} = [15]_{5} = ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDespite this non-uniqueness, we usually choose the smallest nonnegative element of a residue class to represent the class." 13194139534167 17592186045417 false] [17592186412807 1080 "[5]_{8}" 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412807 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412807 1083 #uuid "00a7f57a-39bb-5222-8123-e37237239995" 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412808 1080 "Z" 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412808 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412808 1083 #uuid "02b4d6d9-be4c-597a-93f3-fe7e1d2ff373" 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412809 1080 "[3]_{6}" 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412809 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412809 1083 #uuid "03d0960c-af61-5116-9326-54622ce4d65f" 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412810 1080 "I and III only" 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412810 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412810 1083 #uuid "05f1d139-dd05-5eca-90be-15c2b7b3b5e3" 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412811 1080 "[0]_{5}" 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412811 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412811 1083 #uuid "0636d