3a44078" 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412959 1074 "We have $[a]_{6}=[b]_{6}$ if \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6).\n$$\n\nThe residue class $[-21]_{6}$ consists of the elements that are congruent to $-21$ modulo $6.$ \n\nWe can find an alternative representation for this residue class as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[-21]_{6} & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡-21\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡(-4)\\cdot 6+3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡(-4)\\cdot 0+3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)} \\\\  & =[3]_{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412960 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412940 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412960 1069 "q-214477" 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412960 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412960 1071 "If $[a]_{n}$ denotes the residue class containing all integers congruent to $a$ modulo $n,$ then $[-26]_{6}=$" 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412960 1072 #uuid "0c2846ce-57fa-59ad-9fe2-ab1c6ed9c653" 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412960 1074 "We have $[a]_{6}=[b]_{6}$ if \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6).\n$$\n\nThe residue class $[-26]_{6}$ consists of the elements that are congruent to $-26$ modulo $6.$ \n\nWe can find an alternative representation for this residue class as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[-26]_{6} & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡-26\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡(-5)\\cdot 6+4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡(-5)\\cdot 0+4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)} \\\\  & =[4]_{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412961 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412932 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412961 1069 "q-78640" 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412961 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412961 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- There are $3$ distinct residue classes modulo $6$\n- $[5]_{3}\\cap [1]_{3}\\ne ∅$\n- $Z=[3]_{2}\\cup [6]_{2}$" 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412961 1072 #uuid "0f9012f2-83ba-5f6f-b553-2ea47d5d4f81" 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412961 1074 "Recall that the residue classes modulo $n$ have the following properties:\n\n- There are exactly $n$ distinct residue classes modulo $n$\n\n- Two distinct residue classes have an empty intersection: $[a]_{n}\\cap [b]_{n}=∅$ if $a≢b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- Residue classes modulo $n$ form a partition of the set of all integers: \n\n$$\nZ=[0]_{n}\\cup [1]_{n}\\cup ⋯\\cup [n-1]_{n}\n$$\n\n- The integers modulo $n$ is defined as the set containing all distinct residue classes modulo $n$: \n\n$$\n{Z}_{n}={[0]_{n},\\,[1]_{n},\\,…,\\,[n-1]_{n}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. There are exactly $6$ distinct residue classes modulo $6.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $5≢1\\,(\\text{mod }3),$ we have that $[5]_{3}\\cap [1]_{3}=∅.$\n\n- Statement III is true. First, notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[3]_{2} & =[1+2\\cdot 1]_{2}=[1]_{2} \\\\ [6]_{2} & =[0+2\\cdot 3]_{2}=[0]_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[3]_{2}\\cup [6]_{2} & =[1]_{2}\\cup [0]_{2} \\\\  & =[0]_{2}\\cup [1]_{2} \\\\  & =Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412962 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412949 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412962 1069 "q-86035" 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412962 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412962 1071 "If $[a]_{n}$ denotes the residue class containing all integers congruent to $a$ modulo $n,$ then $[-13]_{5}=$" 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412962 1072 #uuid "1bd78006-ade4-5556-a33a-5b5262685fbe" 13194139534167 175921