 congruent to $17$ modulo $7.$ \n\nWe can find an alternative representation for this residue class as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[16]_{7} & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡16\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡2\\cdot 7+2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡2\\cdot 0+2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)} \\\\  & =[2]_{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412975 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412946 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412975 1069 "q-86027" 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412975 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412975 1071 "Which of the following residue classes modulo $10$ contain the integer $-4?$\n\n- $[4]_{10}$\n- $[-14]_{10}$\n- $[6]_{10}$" 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412975 1072 #uuid "d1a9f6cf-cd8d-55d6-a82c-4176e7807557" 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412975 1074 "We have $-4\\in [a]_{10}$ if \n\n$$\n-4≡a\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10).\n$$\n\nLet's now consider each of the given equivalence classes.\n\n- $-4\\notin [4]_{10}$ because $-4≢4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10)$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- $-4\\in [-14]_{10}$ because $-4≡-14\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10)$ $\\,✓$\n\n- $-4\\in [6]_{10}$ because $-4≡6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10)$ $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412976 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412956 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412976 1069 "q-86021" 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412976 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412976 1071 "Which of the following integers belong to the residue class $[7]_{11}?$\n\n- $18$\n- $-4$\n- $15$" 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412976 1072 #uuid "e91f8fe9-c6f2-558a-bf39-b0389019d5ff" 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412976 1074 "The residue class $[7]_{11}$ consists of the elements that are congruent to $7$ modulo $11.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[7]_{11} & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡7\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11)} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x=7+11k,\\,k\\in Z} \\\\  & ={7+11k\\,|\\,k\\in Z} \\\\  & ={…,\\,7-2\\cdot 11,\\,7-11,\\,7,\\,7+11,\\,7+2\\cdot 11,…} \\\\  & ={…,-15,-4,7,18,29,…}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $-4$ and $18$ belong to $[7]_{11},$ while $15$ does not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412977 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412929 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412977 1069 "q-78644" 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412977 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412977 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- There are $11$ distinct residue classes modulo $11$\n- $[7]_{5}\\cap [2]_{5}\\ne ∅$\n- $Z=[0]_{4}\\cup [9]_{4}\\cup [2]_{4}\\cup [15]_{4}$" 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412977 1072 #uuid "f68491fc-7fbf-551d-bb56-21bac80c159f" 13194139534167 17592186045417 true] [17592186412977 1074 "Recall that the residue classes modulo $n$ have the following properties:\n\n- There are exactly $n$ distinct residue classes modulo $n$\n\n- Two distinct residue classes have an empty intersection: $[a]_{n}\\cap [b]_{n}=∅$ if $a≢b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- Residue classes modulo $n$ form a partition of the set of all integers: \n\n$$\nZ=[0]_{n}\\cup [1]_{n}\\cup ⋯\\cup [n-1]_{n}\n$$\n\n- The integers modulo $n$ is defined as the set containing all distinct residue classes modulo $n$: \n\n$$\n{Z}_{n}={[0]_{n},\\,[1]_{n},\\,…,\\,[n-1]_{n}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. There are $11$ distinct residue classes modulo $11.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since $7≡2\\,(\\text{mod }5),$ we have that $[7]_{5}=[2]_{5}.$ Therefore, $[7]_{5}\\cap [2]_{5}\\ne ∅.$\n\n- Statement III is true. First, notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[9]_{4} & =[1+4\\cdot 2]_{4}=[1]_{4} \\\\ [15]_{4} & =[3+4\\cdot 3]_{4}=[3]_{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0]_{4}\\cup [9]_{4}\\cup [2]_{4}\\cup [15]_{4} & =[0]_{4}\\cup [1]_{4}\\cup [2]_{4}\\cup [3]_{4} \\\\  & =Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." 13194139534167 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-00a7f57a-39bb-5222-8123-e37237239995" 17592186412807 "answer-02b4d6d9-be4c-597a-93f3-fe7e1d2ff373" 17592186412808 "answer-03d0960c-af61-5116-9326-54622ce4d65f" 17592186412809 "answer-05f1d139-dd05-5eca-90be-15c2b7b3b5e3" 17592186412810 "answer-0636d430-5e30-5d21-8647-9ba69f94907c" 17592186412811 "answer-06a4101a-d7b3-5462-979c-883171d6ebfc" 17592186412812 "answer-0c1d27f5-2dd8-5ee0-9c85-fa2aafc19456" 17592186412813 "answer-0df4bc86-a08e-53e9-853a-99dcf0b0d931" 17592186412814 "answer-0e5f0e19-8cbc-59fa-aa4d-6a9680dbc118" 17592186412815 "answer-1033dd79-9600-51c4-a8ca-3a31c3dde48f" 17592186412816 "answer-14030e88-6395-56f9-82ae-0053228a0a90" 17592186412817 "answer-141ec379-3ffa-52e6-bad6-ca1691d2141a" 17592186412818 "answer-16580a0c-365e-59a9-9828-bb02a47e8573" 17592186412819 "answer-19679b71-8be1-5ef4-8058-da70b90df205" 17592186412820 "answer-1fb62df2-30f3-529e-bc16-d7579813742a" 17592186412821 "answer-27688b77-7614-53b0-b9de-691d92f763c7" 17592186412822 "answer-289247b3-ccea-548c-98f4-0b63ed0ff7ef" 17592186412823 "answer-2a74b48c-8a09-5420-9014-3039dfa306ab" 17592186412824 "answer-2ae50f18-3a44-5059-859d-62a308e0626c" 17592186412825 "answer-2c082879-e35e-529b-bf83-498ccc230eb9" 17592186412826 "answer-2d6e5a94-ee4d-53e1-8683-a2c6015c7c95" 17592186412827 "answer-35a6d7e4-0185-5110-8c8f-118bfae37d18" 17592186412828 "answer-37e19197-89ec-5740-a612-329355a01a3c" 17592186412829 "answer-3a9a1c5c-d379-566e-88ec-4f742d23a956" 17592186412830 "answer-3cdd9f0e-932d-558a-9378-6f478cae4f32" 17592186412831 "answer-3ebbd4db-2968-5f79-8339-3f181b9aa536" 17592186412832 "answer-3ef56e58-353f-5a33-bde3-a2b6e4c4903e" 17592186412833 "answer-406d